            "target_entity_instance": "South America",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Nevertheless, satellite observations show that burnt area has declined globally over the last two decades, most notably in the tropical savannas of South America and Africa and grasslands across the Asian steppe. Climate changes, for example, in Central America and the Amazon basin (primarily decreasing dry-day seasonality), and vegetation changes (decreasing gross primary production (GPP)), also drove a reduction in wetter tropical regions of South America (see Figs. 2 and 4). For example, an increase in burnt area is simulated in the tropical regions of South America (see Table 2 and Fig. 2). Larger fires in South America are driven by increased dryness in the tropical rainforests. Given that simulations of future fire activity have not been performed in a systematic way, it is difficult to assess agreement between previous studies over the sign of the global trend in future fire probability.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
